# 2. Promise Chaining

### Question

```js
Promise.resolve(10)
    .then(value => {
        console.log(value);
        return value * 2;
    })
    .then(value => {
        console.log(value);

        throw new Error("Something went wrong");
    })
    .then(value => {
        console.log("Success:", value);
    })
    .catch(error => {
        console.log("Caught:", error.message);
        return 100;
    })
    .then(value => {
        console.log("Final:", value);
    });
```

### Correct output

```text
10
20
Caught: Something went wrong
Final: 100
```

### Flow

```text
10
 ↓
return 20
 ↓
20
 ↓
throw error
 ↓
skip next .then()
 ↓
catch()
 ↓
return 100
 ↓
next .then()
 ↓
Final: 100
```

### Important

When `.then()` throws:

```js
throw new Error("Something went wrong");
```

the Promise returned by that `.then()` becomes rejected.

`catch()` handles it.

Then:

```js
return 100;
```

creates a fulfilled Promise, so the next `.then()` executes.

